// std/constructors.dag -- Type-theoretic constructors (Layer -1).
//
// Product and Coproduct are the irreducible type constructors from which
// ALL other types compose. They are more fundamental than Bit —
// Classical = True | False USES Coproduct, and OrderedRing<T> { ... }
// USES Product.
//
// These are structural axioms: they exist before any .dag file can be
// parsed, because parsing itself produces Product and Coproduct nodes.
// Declaring them here makes the ontology explicit and allows the compiler
// to reference them from DAG structure rather than a hardcoded enum.
//
// STRUCTURAL SEMANTICS:
//
//   Product (Conj):
//     All children hold simultaneously.
//     Denotational: |A × B| = |A| · |B| (cartesian product)
//     Examples: record fields, tuple elements, struct members
//     A product node with N children means "all N values are present."
//
//   Coproduct (Disj):
//     Exactly one child holds.
//     Denotational: |A + B| = |A| + |B| (disjoint union)
//     Examples: enum variants, sum types, tagged unions
//     A coproduct node with N children means "exactly 1 of N is selected."
//
//   Terminal (NoConnective):
//     Zero-factor composition. A leaf node.
//     Denotational: |1| = 1 (single inhabitant)
//     Examples: Unit type, primitive types (Int, Bool without looking inside)
//
// CARDINALITY:
//
//   Cardinality is orthogonal to constructors. A binding site can be:
//     Required (exactly 1 value)
//     Optional (0 or 1 value)
//   This is annotation on the binding, not a type wrapper.
//
// THE COMPILER'S VIEW:
//
//   The compiler knows only three structural shapes:
//     - Product (all children): traverse all, merge results
//     - Coproduct (one child): branch, exhaustiveness check
//     - Terminal (leaf): base case
//
//   Everything else — what Int means, what String means, what methods
//   a type has — is DAG composition from algebra.dag, not compiler knowledge.
//
// CURRENT REPRESENTATION:
//
//   type Connective = Conj | Disj | NoConnective
//   (defined historically as a compiler-internal enum in the legacy core DAG)
//
//   This enum IS the Layer -1 vocabulary. The dissolution path:
//   when the compiler reads Product/Coproduct from std declarations,
//   the Connective enum dissolves. The 313 sites that read .connective
//   and match on Conj/Disj become structural reads on std-declared
//   constructor types.
//
// NOTE: This file declares the ontology. The compiler-side dissolution
// of the Connective enum is tracked in the ROADMAP under
// "Type Constructors (Layer -1, not yet in std)."

module std.constructors

// Product: all children hold simultaneously.
// The type of "and" composition.
type Product

// Coproduct: exactly one child holds.
// The type of "or" composition.
type Coproduct

// Terminal: zero-factor composition. Single-inhabitant phantom witness.
// Used by type-level phantom composition carriers (e.g. Compose<Algebra, MachineConstraint>).
type Phantom

// Cardinality: presence annotation on binding sites.
type Cardinality = Required | Optional
